{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1944 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "466b031ad5a06a88f927cc3c45e96992ef6941191d2874485986244d65f69441" :edb/db-before-t 1943 :edb/db-after-t 1944 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535256 50 #inst "2026-10-13T10:08:27.427Z" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186054659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487383 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186054775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487384 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063354 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063356 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063944 1071 "Given that $12 - 5m = 3m$, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063944 1071 "Given that $12 - 5m = 3m$, what is the value of $m?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063944 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nNotice that we have $-5m$ on the left-hand side and $3m$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $-5m$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{blue}5m}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 - 5m &= 3m \\\\\n12 - 5m + 5m &= 3m + 5m \\\\\n12 &= 8m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 8m \\\\\n\\frac{12}{8} &= \\frac{8m}{8} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{2} &= \\frac{8m}{8} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{2} &= m \\\\\nm &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063944 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $-5m$ on the left-hand side and $3m$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $-5m$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{blue}5m}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-5m & =3m \\\\ 12-5m+5m & =3m+5m \\\\ 12 & =8m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =8m \\\\ \\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{8m}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}m}{\\cancel{8}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =m \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063945 1071 "Solve the equation $3x-12 = 5x + 8$." 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063945 1071 "Solve the equation $3x-12 = 5x + 8.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063945 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nNotice that we have $3x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $3x$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}3x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 12 &= 5x + 8 \\\\\n3x - 12 - 3x &= 5x + 8 - 3x \\\\\n- 12 &= 2x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $8$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{red}8}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12 &= 2x + 8 \\\\\n- 12 - 8 &= 2x + 8 - 8 \\\\\n- 20 &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20 &= 2x \\\\\n\\frac{-20}{2} &= \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\\n- 10 &= \\frac{2x}{2} \\\\\n- 10 &= x \\\\\nx &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063945 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $3x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $3x$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}3x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-12 & =5x+8 \\\\ 3x-12-3x & =5x+8-3x \\\\ -12 & =2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $8$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{red}8}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =2x+8 \\\\ -12-8 & =2x+8-8 \\\\ -20 & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{-20}{2} & =\\frac{2x}{2} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ -10 & =x \\\\ x & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063946 1071 "Solve for $t$ where $2(t+3)= 5t-3$." 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063946 1071 "Solve for $t$ where $2(t+3)= 5t-3.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186063946 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses on the left-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(t + 3) &= 5t - 3 \\\\\n2 \\cdot t + 2 \\cdot 3 &= 5t - 3 \\\\\n2t + 6 &= 5t - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nNotice that we have $2t$ on the left-hand side and $5t$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $2t$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}2t}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t + 6 &= 5t - 3 \\\\\n2t + 6 - 2t &= 5t - 3 - 2t \\\\\n6 &= 3t - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}3}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 3t - 3 \\\\\n6 + 3 &= 3t - 3 + 3 \\\\\n9 &= 3t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 &= 3t \\\\\n\\frac{9}{3} &= \\frac{3t}{3} \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{3t}{3} \\\\\n3 &= t \\\\\nt &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186063946 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses on the left-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t+3) & =5t-3 \\\\ 2\\cdot t+2\\cdot 3 & =5t-3 \\\\ 2t+6 & =5t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $2t$ on the left-hand side and $5t$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $2t$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}2t}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+6 & =5t-3 \\\\ 2t+6-2t & =5t-3-2t \\\\ 6 & =3t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}3}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3t-3 \\\\ 6+3 & =3t-3+3 \\\\ 9 & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =3t \\\\ \\frac{9}{3} & =\\frac{3t}{3} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}t}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 3 & =t \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487398 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487343 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487379 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064344 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487351 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064344 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487397 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064345 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487360 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064345 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487371 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064346 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487376 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064346 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487401 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064434 1050 480.0 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064883 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = 4x - \\sin x$ and the point $P(0, 0)$ that lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, calculate $(f^{-1})^{′}(0)$." 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186064883 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186064883 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186065419 1064 "To solve a linear equation with variables on both sides, we bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n5x = 2x + 12\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $5x$ on the left-hand side and $2x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $\\color{blue}2x$ from the right-hand side. We can do this by *subtracting* it from both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= 2x + 12 \\\\\n5x - 2x &= 2x + 12 - 2x \\\\\n3x &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x &= 12 \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=4$ is our answer, and we're done.\n\nWe can check that $x=4$ is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= ^(?)2x + 12 \\\\\n5(4) &= ^(?)2(4) + 12 \\\\\n20 &= 20✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186065419 1064 "To solve a linear equation with variables on both sides, we bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n5x = 2x + 12\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that we have $5x$ on the left-hand side and $2x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $\\color{blue}2x$ from the right-hand side. We can do this by *subtracting* it from both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =2x+12 \\\\ 5x-2x & =2x+12-2x \\\\ 3x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=4$ is our answer, and we're done. \n\nWe can check that $x=4$ is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & \\overset{?}{=}2x+12 \\\\ 5(4) & \\overset{?}{=}2(4)+12 \\\\ 20 & =20\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186065420 1064 "Let's now solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n9x -9= 1+4x\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nNotice that we have $9x$ on the left-hand side and $4x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $4x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}4x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 9 &= 1 + 4x \\\\\n9x - 9 - 4x &= 1 + 4x - 4x \\\\\n5x - 9 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-9$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}9}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 9 &= 1 \\\\\n5x - 9 + 9 &= 1 + 9 \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{10}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is our answer. We can check that this is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 9 &= 1 + 4x \\\\\n9(2) - 9 &= ^(?)1 + 4(2) \\\\\n18 - 9 &= ^(?)9 \\\\\n9 &= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186065420 1064 "Let's now solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n9x -9= 1+4x\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $9x$ on the left-hand side and $4x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $4x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}4x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-9 & =1+4x \\\\ 9x-9-4x & =1+4x-4x \\\\ 5x-9 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-9$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}9}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-9 & =1 \\\\ 5x-9+9 & =1+9 \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =10 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is our answer. We can check that this is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-9 & =1+4x \\\\ 9(2)-9 & \\overset{?}{=}1+4(2) \\\\ 18-9 & \\overset{?}{=}9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186066121 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486924 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186066121 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486924 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186066122 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486841 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186066122 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486841 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186068475 1071 "Solve $2x^2 - 27=5$." 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186068475 1071 "Solve $2x^2 - 27=5.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186068475 1074 "First, we isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 27 &= 5 \\\\\n2x^{2} &= 32 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x^{2}} &= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n\\mid x \\mid &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186068475 1074 "First, we isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-27 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186068476 1071 "Solve for $p$ where $4p^2 - 11 = 14$." 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186068476 1071 "Solve for $p$ where $4p^2 - 11 = 14.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186068476 1074 "First, we isolate the $p^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4p^{2} - 11 &= 14 \\\\\n4p^{2} &= 25 \\\\\np^{2} &= \\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting abso