 equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n$2x^{2} - 18|= 0; 2x^{2}|= 18; x^{2}|= 9$\n2. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n$\\sqrt{x^{2}}|= \\sqrt{9}; \\mid x \\mid |= 3$\n3. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n$x|= 3orx =-3$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3$. We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3$, where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186070019 1064 "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186070024 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\mid x+1 \\mid = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n$x + 1|= 4; x + 1 - 1|= 4 - 1; x|= 3$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n$- (x + 1)|= 4; - x - 1|= 4; - x - 1 + 1|= 4 + 1; - x|= 5; x|=-5$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}{-5}}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186070024 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{ or } \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =4-1 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+1) & =4 \\\\ -x-1 & =4 \\\\ -x-1+1 & =4+1 \\\\ -x & =5 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186070025 1064 "So, we've seen that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid x+1 \\mid = 4\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}{-5}}\n$$\n\nWe can verify that these solutions are correct by substituting them back into the original equation and checking we get a true statement:\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{blue}3}$ into our equation, we get\n$\\mid x + 1 \\mid |= 4; \\mid 3 + 1 \\mid |= 4; \\mid 4 \\mid |= 4; 4|= 4.✓$\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n- Substituting $x={\\