00 \\\\\n3z + 25 - 25 &= 100 - 25 \\\\\n3z &= 75 \\\\\n\\frac{3z}{3} &= \\frac{75}{3} \\\\\nz &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186138079 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25+0.03z & =1 \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.25+0.03z) & =100\\cdot (1) \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.25)+100\\cdot (0.03z) & =100 \\\\ 25+3z & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25+3z & =100 \\\\ 3z+25 & =100 \\\\ 3z+25-25 & =100-25 \\\\ 3z & =75 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}z}{\\cancel{3}} & =\\frac{75}{3} \\\\ z & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138526 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487297 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138526 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487347 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138527 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487313 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138527 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487356 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138528 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487301 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138528 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487372 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186138597 1050 420.0 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186139664 1064 "To solve an equation with a decimal coefficient, such as\n$0.2x = -1$,\n\nwe start by getting rid of the decimal. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${\\color{blue}10}$. Multiplying a decimal by $10$ shifts the decimal place one spot over to the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2x &= -1 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (0.2x) &= 10 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n2x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= -10 \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{-10}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= -\\frac{10}{2} \\\\\nx &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186139664 1064 "To solve an equation with a decimal coefficient, such as \n\n$$\n0.2x = -1,\n$$\n\nwe start by getting rid of the decimal. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${\\color{blue}10}.$ Multiplying a decimal by $10$ shifts the decimal place one spot over to the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2x & =-1 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.2x) & =10\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{-10}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =-\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186139665 1064 "How do we get rid of the decimal in an equation like\n\n$$\n0.25x = 2\n$$\n\nwhen there is more than one number behind the decimal place? If we multiply by $10$ as usual, we shift the decimal place over one spot to the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25x &= 2 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (0.25x) &= 10 \\cdot (2) \\\\\n2.5x &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is still one number behind the decimal place. So, we can multiply by $10$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2.5x &= 20 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (2.5x) &= 10 \\cdot (20) \\\\\n25x &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that our equation contains only whole numbers, we can continue with the usual method of applying the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x &= 200 \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking back, we can see a faster way to apply the same method. We multiplied by $10$ twice, which means that overall, we multiplied by $100$. In general, multiplying a decimal by $100$ shifts the decimal place over two spots to the right. So, we could have solved the equation faster by multiplying by $100$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25x &= 2 \\\\\n100 \\cdot (0.25x) &= 100 \\cdot (2) \\\\\n25x &= 200 \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186139665 1064 "How do we get rid of the decimal in an equation like\n\n$$\n0.25x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhen there is more than one number behind the decimal place? If we multiply by $10$ as usual, we shift the decimal place over one spot to the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25x & =2 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.25x) & =10\\cdot (2) \\\\ 2.5x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is still one number behind the decimal place. So, we can multiply by $10$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5x & =20 \\\\ 10\\cdot (2.5x) & =10\\cdot (20) \\\\ 25x & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that our equation contains only whole numbers, we can continue with the usual method of applying the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25x & =200 \\\\ \\frac{25x}{25} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{25}x}{\\cancel{25}} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking back, we can see a faster way to apply the same method. We multiplied by $10$ twice, which means that overall, we multiplied by $100.$ In general, multiplying a decimal by $100$ shifts the decimal place over two spots to the right. So, we could have solved the equation faster by multiplying by $100.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25x & =2 \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.25x) & =100\\cdot (2) \\\\ 25x & =200 \\\\ \\frac{25x}{25} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{25}x}{\\cancel{25}} & =\\frac{200}{25} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186147563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487303 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155974 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155975 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155976 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155977 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155978 13194139535256 17592186045417 false] [17592186155973 1075 #edb/