39535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487287 1084 #edb/ref 17592186487452 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487287 1085 "![](images/f8/f82a8a9f2c830772788bd5c49bf39cd7a5ada93bcab1f81fa08f247c6020d33c.png)\n\nPaul owns a rectangular plot of land, as shown above. The area of the plot is $3{x}^{2}+15x$ square yards, and its width is $x+5$ yards, where $x>10$ is a fixed constant." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487287 1086 #uuid "51a2d6a6-b60f-50cd-97ea-595fbb919894" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487287 1087 #edb/ref 17592186487449 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487242 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1069 "q-154647" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1071 "If $|4-y|=5,$ then $y=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1072 #uuid "00c374c7-fe58-5f15-8b69-0d5871097d1a" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487288 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|4-y|=4-y,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-y| & =5 \\\\ 4-y & =5 \\\\ -y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|4-y|=-(4-y),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-y| & =5 \\\\ -(4-y) & =5 \\\\ -4+y & =5 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=9.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487237 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1069 "q-218607" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{{a}^{2}-5}{3}=0.$ Express your final answer in its simplest form.\n\n$a=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1072 #uuid "01fa67fa-62b9-56b9-929f-65394494b5c0" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487289 1074 "First, we isolate the ${a}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{2}-5}{3} & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-5 & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ a & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487182 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1069 "q-154585" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1071 "If $|z+7|=1,$ then $z=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1072 #uuid "02ee9945-dcfc-5770-8779-f9ff7dbf59ba" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487290 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|z+7|=z+7,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|z+7| & =1 \\\\ z+7 & =1 \\\\ z+7-7 & =1-7 \\\\ z & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|z+7|=-(z+7),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|z+7| & =1 \\\\ -(z+7) & =1 \\\\ -z-7 & =1 \\\\ -z-7+7 & =1+7 \\\\ -z & =8 \\\\ z & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=-8$ and $z=-6.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487234 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1069 "q-84845" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+4)^{2}=-3(x-3)?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1072 #uuid "0668c7c6-b240-56da-94fb-07d6ea371e42" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487291 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-4).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+4=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+4=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487229 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1069 "q-350955" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1072 #uuid "11666770-4f2b-5054-898a-6ed32728d68e" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487292 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=2{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,dx=\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{2({e}^{2})^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2(1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{3}]-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{6}}{3}-(\\frac{2{e}^{6}}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(12{e}^{6}-2{e}^{6}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(5{e}^{6}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487191 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1069 "q-107861" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1071 "$2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1072 #uuid "1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487293 1074 "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487142 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1069 "q-253513" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1072 #uuid "13b42a92-bc61-5733-b661-85ce69e84b97" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487294 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/35/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487169 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1069 "q-148664" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1071 "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two light bulb types: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $2%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $50%$ of the entire production, and $3%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $25%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, and $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1072 #uuid "1462b070-fed9-586f-924d-db4fb0fa8a6a" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487295 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487147 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1069 "q-218731" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1071 "Find $u$ if  ${u}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1072 #uuid "16965fbd-f380-5aaa-bd39-b9dfc41f6cc7" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487296 1074 "First, we isolate the ${u}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {u}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ {u}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{10}-\\frac{4}{10} \\\\ {u}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\ |u| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\ |u| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ |u| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ u & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487243 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1069 "q-180226" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1071 "If $0.6y=-4.8$, then $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1072 #uuid "18f83573-c25e-51a6-ba43-48ece64ab68b" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487297 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6y & =-4.8 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.6y) & =10\\cdot (-4.8) \\\\ 6y & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{6y}{6} & =\\frac{-48}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}y}{\\cancel{6}} & =-8 \\\\ y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487173 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1069 "q-253896" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1071 "![](images/0d/0d745d274250c84f6701ff22d17d0c6506a9f03f4a1a461e09f609a0ea11d99b.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1072 #uuid "1a350bae-823f-50ee-90ca-2add36d5d3d7" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487298 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487280 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1069 "q-154631" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1071 "If $|2y+3|=9,$ then $y=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1072 #uuid "1a74fe55-d2b0-52cd-a9aa-4309f72efe42" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487299 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|2y+3|=2y+3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+3| & =9 \\\\ 2y+3 & =9 \\\\ 2y & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2y+3|=-(2y+3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+3| & =9 \\\\ -(2y+3) & =9 \\\\ -2y-3 & =9 \\\\ -2y & =12 \\\\ \\frac{-2y}{-2} & =\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-6$ and $y=3.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487177 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1069 "q-30226" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1071 "Solve for $t$ where ${t}^{2}=\\frac{16}{9}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1072 #uuid "1b387dd0-04fb-5792-afe0-da3fe94240d2" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487300 1074 "We take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |t| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ |t| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487270 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1069 "q-180238" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1071 "If $0.52t=0.4t+2.4$, then $t=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1072 #uuid "1e95a376-82a0-5b4f-a431-8c6e09301595" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487301 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.52t & =0.4t+2.4 \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.52t) & =100\\cdot (0.4t+2.4) \\\\ 52t & =40t+240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52t & =40t+240 \\\\ 52t-40t & =40t+240-40t \\\\ 12t & =240 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}t}{\\cancel{12}} & =\\frac{240}{12} \\\\ t & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487149 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1069 "q-107851" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1071 "Evaluate $Γ(8).$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1072 #uuid "1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487302 1074 "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(8)=7!=5\\,040.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487235 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1069 "q-133799" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1071 "The value $V(t)$ of Paul's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=400\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nPaul's land grew in value by\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first two years." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1072 #uuid "2064e26a-7656-5c06-9eb5-86690541ec19" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487303 1074 "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =400\\cdot (1.1)^{(2)} \\\\  & =400\\cdot (1.21) \\\\  & =484\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =484-400 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was $84$ thousand dollars." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487261 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1069 "q-175605" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1071 "If $\\frac{10-3x}{4}=1$, then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1072 #uuid "20bfc5d7-9144-5859-b1f0-27af1f1cfa84" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487304 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-3x}{4} & =1 \\\\ 4(\\frac{10-3x}{4}) & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{4}(\\frac{10-3x}{\\cancel{4}}) & =4 \\\\ 10-3x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-3x & =4 \\\\ 10-3x-10 & =4-10 \\\\ -3x & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & =\\frac{-6}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}} & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487180 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487186 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1069 "q-323132" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-1=3t,\\,y+4={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1072 #uuid "2210e304-f1be-5ab9-a627-e9a4a8410a48" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487305 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-1}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y+4$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =y+4 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =9(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487192 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1069 "q-147889" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.75, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.15, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.8, & P({A}_{3})=0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1072 #uuid "25486da2-4aed-57cd-8065-4f05b0f0cbe6" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487306 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.3+0.6) \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{(0.75)(0.1)+(0.15)(0.3)+(0.8)(0.6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487174 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1069 "q-351024" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1072 #uuid "2827cc9e-45ac-55e8-ad01-41ef4d905442" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487307 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x(\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{3})^{2})\\,({e}^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})\\,(1)]-2{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6})-2x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}-2({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487198 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1069 "q-218546" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1071 "If ${x}^{2}-15=34,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1072 #uuid "28369a0e-aba7-51eb-8715-45d32f7550f3" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487308 1074 "First, we isolate the ${x}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-15 & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ |x| & =7 \\\\ x & =\\pm 7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487225 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1069 "q-178530" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1071 "If $5y-7=\\frac{16-5y}{2},$ then $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1072 #uuid "2de1d7cb-6ad2-5a64-8b22-82ed6e525706" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487309 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2.$ \nWe must make sure that all of the terms on the left-hand side are multiplied by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =\\frac{16-5y}{2} \\\\ 2(5y-7) & =2(\\frac{16-5y}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5y+2\\cdot (-7) & =\\cancel{2}(\\frac{16-5y}{\\cancel{2}}) \\\\ 10y-14 & =16-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10y-14 & =16-5y \\\\ 10y-14+5y & =16-5y+5y \\\\ 15y-14 & =16 \\\\ 15y-14+14 & =16+14 \\\\ 15y & =30 \\\\ \\frac{15y}{15} & =\\frac{30}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{15}y}{\\cancel{15}} & =\\frac{30}{15} \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487247 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1069 "q-106454" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-2,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-10,-5)?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1072 #uuid "2f7086f8-f36f-50c7-ad0c-2ef637d5cf6f" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487310 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,-5),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=-5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-3=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487175 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1069 "q-132425" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1071 "![](images/79/797cddb4ea3cb41959df88e56517e9ee71e76e3cd08a9a93d2957b9792d14abb.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}12x\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1072 #uuid "307a3f33-b63b-5a72-91e6-852f4ed601a8" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487311 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0e/0e69b1cb480b87216273db4336adff57cbf9d9bc450aac4b0cdb58b1ae63f6f7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{2x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{4x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12x\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}12x\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}12x\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}12x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}12x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}12xy\\,{|}_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}12xy\\,{|}_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}12x[\\frac{x}{3}+3+\\frac{2x}{3}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}12x[\\frac{x}{3}+3-\\frac{4x}{3}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}12x(x+3)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}12x(3-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(12{x}^{2}+36x)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(36x-12{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}){|}_{-3}^{0}+(18{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(4(-3)^{3}+18(-3)^{2})+18(3)^{2}-4(3)^{3} \\\\  & =108-162+162-108 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487256 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1069 "q-133795" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1071 "What is the degree of the polynomial that represents the area of Karol's plot of land?\n\nDegree $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1072 #uuid "309a6ab7-981e-5132-839c-89444347093a" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487312 1074 "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-8\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2{x}^{1}-8{x}^{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, it has the exponents $2,$ $1,$ and $0.$ The degree is the largest of these exponents, which is $2.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487184 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1069 "q-102055" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1071 "If $0.07c=2.8$, then $c=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1072 #uuid "316ee768-f13c-53c1-a1ba-cd88e7e69557" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487313 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.07c & =2.8 \\\\ 100\\cdot (0.07c) & =100\\cdot (2.8) \\\\ 7c & =280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7c & =280 \\\\ \\frac{7c}{7} & =\\frac{280}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}c}{\\cancel{7}} & =\\frac{280}{7} \\\\ c & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487216 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1069 "q-101638" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{7w+10}{3}=4w.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1072 #uuid "31ff821e-8692-536f-977f-3df1a10cce49" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487314 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7w+10}{3} & =4w \\\\ 3(\\frac{7w+10}{3}) & =3\\cdot (4w) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{7w+10}{\\cancel{3}}) & =12w \\\\ 7w+10 & =12w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7w+10 & =12w \\\\ 7w+10-7w & =12w-7w \\\\ 10 & =5w \\\\ \\frac{10}{5} & =\\frac{5w}{5} \\\\ 2 & =w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $w=2.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487208 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1069 "q-30481" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1072 #uuid "328d9940-84c5-5488-9fa8-98ff5e7ef880" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487315 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use a substitution $x+1=u,$ thus $x=u-1$ and $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\int \\frac{u-1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\int \\,\\text{d}u+\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-u+\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=D-1$ is an integral constant." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487248 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1069 "q-27340" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1071 "![](images/9c/9c14d7a4f9a460c3219cb457ff9f8ba8fb32a22101dfba3e0925aa8077b011cf.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠ABC$ is an angle and $D$ is a point in the interior of $∠ABC$ such that $m∠CBD=5x+{3}^{∘}.$ If $m∠ABC={106}^{∘}$ and $∠CBD≅∠ABD$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1072 #uuid "38145db5-f11d-5939-a57b-880dc1abb8bc" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487316 1074 "By the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\nm∠ABC=m∠ABD+m∠CBD.\n$$\n\nSince $∠CBD≅∠ABD$, then we have $m∠CBD=m∠ABD.$  Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ABC & =m∠ABD+m∠CBD \\\\  & =m∠CBD+m∠CBD \\\\  & =2m∠CBD.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $m∠CBD=5x+{3}^{∘}$ and $m∠ABC={106}^{∘},$ we can solve for $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{106}^{∘} & =2(5x+3) \\\\ {106}^{∘} & =10x+{6}^{∘} \\\\ {100}^{∘} & =10x \\\\ {10}^{∘} & =x \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487156 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1069 "q-350948" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1072 #uuid "3c9f18f7-85c5-5539-81bc-9088568cf461" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487317 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}={x}^{-2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{-2}\\,dx=-{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(-{x}^{-1}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(-{x}^{-1})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{{e}^{2}}-(-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{1})]+{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{{e}^{2}}+(-{x}^{-1}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}+(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487269 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1069 "q-23833" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1071 "Find $z$ if ${z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{50}=0.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1072 #uuid "40c2d4a4-7047-536a-96ef-5761a02b86db" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487318 1074 "First, we isolate the ${z}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{50} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{50}} \\\\ |z| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{50}} \\\\ |z| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{50}} \\\\ |z| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2}} \\\\ |z| & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487250 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1069 "q-148270" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1071 "An English teacher believes that students who prefer print handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove her belief, she puts some students into three groups according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in print.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $55%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $52%$ of those students usually write in print.\n\nIf $42%$ of students are female, $80%$ of female students write in print, and $56%$ of students that write in print are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1072 #uuid "42158c74-7958-54df-8e89-80fc77114095" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487319 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that a student writes in print, and $L,A,H$ be the event that a student has low, average, or high writing grades, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.55 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females write in print, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students that write in print are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|W)=0.56.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student with low grades writes in print, which is represented as $P(W|L).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\ P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.42)}{P(W|L)(0.2)+(0.55)(0.65)+(0.52)(0.15)} \\\\ 0.56 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.2P(W|L)+0.4355}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W|L),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56(0.2P(W|L)+0.4355) & =0.336 \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{0.336}{0.56} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L)+0.4355 & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0.2P(W|L) & =\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355 \\\\ P(W|L) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{3}{5}-0.4355) \\\\ P(W|L) & ≈0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487213 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1069 "q-103282" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1071 "![](images/6f/6fe8fc042cc8d5645e9cf0df2fd297e018504c4248a45d348e4778a14caea9ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the angles shown above?\n\nI. $m∠AOB=m∠DOE$\n\nII. $m∠BOC=m∠COD$\n\nIII. $m∠BOC=m∠EOF$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1072 #uuid "432febe7-1220-5082-90e3-bf3d1c64fe2c" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487320 1074 "From the diagram, we see that \n\n- $∠AOB$ and $∠DOE$ are marked with $|$,\n\n- $∠BOC$ and $∠COD$ are marked with $||$, and\n\n- $∠EOF$ is marked with $|||.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOB & ≅∠DOE \\\\ ∠BOC & ≅∠COD.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠DOE \\\\ m∠BOC & =m∠COD.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo statements I and II are true, while statement III is false." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487183 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1069 "q-147882" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{8}, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=1, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{7}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1072 #uuid "43f08f68-330a-59ee-956f-33e7cbacb451" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487321 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{8})(\\frac{2}{5})+(1)(\\frac{7}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{20})}{(\\frac{9}{20})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487153 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1069 "q-132389" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ and the lines $y=5-2x$ and $y=-\\frac{x}{3}.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1072 #uuid "44184e7a-6e43-56fd-ad13-4a3507c85f19" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487322 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & \\,⟹\\,x=2\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=5-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{x}{3} & \\,⟹\\,x=-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/b0/b0f6f95d81c665d189d1b1e97f78b82b6c64abd5c1ae17ffe4f918e7650a7bcc.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/87/873c2a14782c1ceb96fc3c1c71888dd221761d21749824e39157671979a62f0f.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,-3y\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{y}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{y}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-3y}^{5/2-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{y}}^{5/2-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487230 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1069 "q-148667" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1071 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ buys $33%$ of the copies, and $90%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $2$ buys $25%$ of the copies, and $87%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ buys $42%$ of the copies, and $82%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $5%$ of all copies have some printing defect, and $70%$ of defective copies are sold, what is the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1072 #uuid "47b23978-faf0-57d0-85ed-6efe7d74f281" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487323 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is bought by distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.33 &  & P(S|{D}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.87 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.42 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.33+0.25+0.42=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.05.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect, which is represented as $P(R|S).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.05)}{(0.9)(0.33)+(0.87)(0.25)+(0.82)(0.42)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.035}{0.8589} \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487233 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1069 "q-133796" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1071 "If $x=20\\,\\text{yd},$ what is the area of Karol's land plot?\n\nArea $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{yd}}^{2}$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1072 #uuid "4b044a91-329e-59b9-adc9-5aa3764882b6" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487324 1074 "To find the area, we have to evaluate the polynomial ${x}^{2}+2x-8$ at $x=20.$\n\nSo, we substitute $x=20$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & = \\\\ (20)^{2}+2(20)-8 & = \\\\ 400+40-8 & = \\\\ 432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is $432\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487151 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487207 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1069 "q-215133" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1071 "Written in **ascending order**, the solutions to $|1-y|=8$ are $y=${{field-1}} and $y=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1072 #uuid "5182296b-2876-594b-b8ec-d9a9269a4652" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487325 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|1-y|=1-y,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-y| & =8 \\\\ 1-y & =8 \\\\ -y & =7 \\\\ y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|1-y|=-(1-y),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-y| & =8 \\\\ -(1-y) & =8 \\\\ -1+y & =8 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $y=-7$ and $y=9.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487138 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1069 "q-148152" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1072 #uuid "521e5a18-fa22-58cf-9f82-7b4aa14a1323" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487326 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487160 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1069 "q-107128" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1071 "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of an exhaust pipe of a motorcycle can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe is ${140}^{∘}F,$ calculate its temperature after $3$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1072 #uuid "533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487327 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the exhaust pipe of a motorcycle is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-74| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-74>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-74 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-74 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-74 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =74+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe was ${140}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =74+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =74+K \\\\ K & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=74+66{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the exhaust pipe after $t=3$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(3)=74+66{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 3}≈{126}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487255 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1069 "q-253541" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1071 "What is the infimum of the set $B=\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1072 #uuid "546541ff-37d6-5bb0-b37e-29f5b9d47681" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487328 1074 "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}=-0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487251 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1069 "q-252987" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1071 "What is the supremum of the set $A=\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1072 #uuid "56e0e5d9-1f02-588c-913a-41be6c70db4a" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487329 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487164 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1069 "q-52667" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1071 "From the information given below, find all of the angles that are congruent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & ={30}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & ={150}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠D & ={180}^{∘}-m∠C, \\\\ m∠E & ={120}^{∘}+m∠A, \\\\ m∠F & =m∠E.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1072 #uuid "5707b071-c7c6-5712-89ca-e66cbdfb1c32" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487330 1074 "First, let's find the measures of $∠D$, $∠E$ and $∠F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠D & ={180}^{∘}-m∠C \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={0}^{∘} \\\\  \\\\ m∠E & ={120}^{∘}+m∠A \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘} \\\\  \\\\ m∠F & =m∠E={150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & ={30}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={0}^{∘} \\\\ m∠E & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ m∠F & ={150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have that $m∠B=m∠E=m∠F.$ Therefore, $∠B≅∠E≅∠F.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487218 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1069 "q-171596" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1071 "If $\\frac{3+m}{7}=2m-7,$ then $m=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1072 #uuid "595c6cf3-442f-5ee5-b303-59b7320fc44a" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487331 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+m}{7} & =2m-7 \\\\ 7(\\frac{3+m}{7}) & =7(2m-7) \\\\ \\cancel{7}(\\frac{3+m}{\\cancel{7}}) & =14m-49 \\\\ 3+m & =14m-49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+m & =14m-49 \\\\ 3+m-m & =14m-49-m \\\\ 3 & =13m-49 \\\\ 3+49 & =13m-49+49 \\\\ 52 & =13m \\\\ \\frac{52}{13} & =\\frac{13m}{13} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{\\cancel{13}m}{\\cancel{13}} \\\\ 4 & =m \\\\ m & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487150 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1069 "q-253899" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1071 "![](images/56/567aa249f2ed84b537d20d4137a28e0df1cd3e4c942f4cf305bd864da32b8bac.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1072 #uuid "5b171f8f-d26a-5943-99d9-8e4c4110d0e4" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487332 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487279 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1069 "q-154662" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1071 "Which of the following equations has *no* solution?\n\nI. $|2x+1|-2=3$\n\nII. $|2x+1|=1$\n\nIII. $|2x+1|=-3$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1072 #uuid "5bd06631-7e99-59a6-a0d1-351c08d361fa" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487333 1074 "An absolute value equation has no solution if, when the absolute value is isolated on the left side, the right-hand side is negative.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each equation in turn:\n\n- Equation I has a solution. To see this, we first isolate the absolute value on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+1|-2 & =3 \\\\ |2x+1| & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side is non-negative, this equation has a solution.\n\n- Equation II has a solution. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is non-negative.\n\n- Equation III *has no solution*. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487267 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1069 "q-133798" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1071 "Paul purchased his land for $400$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Paul's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1072 #uuid "5c2f07e7-1da9-5f1b-b117-db8d520999a1" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487334 1074 "The land initially costs $400$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars \n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=644.204≈644,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $644$ thousand dollars." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487168 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1069 "q-154697" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1071 "If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=2,$ then $x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1072 #uuid "5d250407-c3da-5691-8698-d38c853f8bfd" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487335 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=2-\\frac{3}{4}x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-\\frac{3}{4}x| & =2 \\\\ 2-\\frac{3}{4}x & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\frac{4}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}x & =0 \\\\ (-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}x) & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (0) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{4}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{3}{4}}x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=-(2-\\frac{3}{4}x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-\\frac{3}{4}x| & =2 \\\\ -(2-\\frac{3}{4}x) & =2 \\\\ -2+\\frac{3}{4}x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\frac{4}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x & =4 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}x) & =(\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (4) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{4}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{3}{4}}x) & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{16}{3}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487155 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1069 "q-253550" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5-n}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1072 #uuid "5d279c4a-988e-5c29-a6a0-5c2bb436ded0" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487336 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-0} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-1} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-3} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487268 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1069 "q-82359" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=2x,$ $y=5-3x$ and $x=0.$\nExpress ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1072 #uuid "5f7eccff-14bb-598f-9945-41d3d8682a45" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487337 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=5-3x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{5-y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/80/805f6d6117e6c9028cb63f1ff8328bc625dc992a1f270abecdf898df31204613.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/b3/b394a5a80ab4fafdcc7e5457f37360d693ddb7c24e1f6ed208ada3b778b3623c.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 5,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{5-y}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{(5-y)/3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487145 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487202 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487227 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487264 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487265 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487275 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1069 "q-253560" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1071 "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1072 #uuid "60981b2b-035b-5158-a36c-3b0a5f268ae1" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487338 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=+\\infty .$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487278 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1069 "q-154635" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1071 "Consider the absolute value equation \n\n$$\n5-|x-3|=-3.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$$\n|x-3|=k\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1072 #uuid "65b91c89-ea1f-5d94-8a2d-584db112f3d8" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487339 1074 "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we subtract $5$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-|x-3|=-3 \\\\ 5-|x-3|-5=-3-5 \\\\ -|x-3|=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|x-3| & =-8 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-|x-3|) & =(-1)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ (\\cancel{-}1)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}|x-3|) & =8 \\\\ |x-3| & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=8.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487158 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1069 "q-101643" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1071 "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{2n-8}{3}=-6(n+1).$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1072 #uuid "6dc17e55-1c4a-5a93-b3b8-937d5766223d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487340 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2n-8}{3} & =-6(n+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2n-8}{3}) & =3\\cdot (-6(n+1)) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2n-8}{\\cancel{3}}) & =-18(n+1) \\\\ 2n-8 & =-18n-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n-8 & =-18n-18 \\\\ 2n-8+18n & =-18n-18+18n \\\\ 20n-8 & =-18 \\\\ 20n-8+8 & =-18+8 \\\\ 20n & =-10 \\\\ \\frac{20n}{20} & =\\frac{-10}{20} \\\\ n & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ n & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487144 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1069 "q-44323" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{7-2x}{4}=3x.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1072 #uuid "7153151e-24e5-5164-807d-f6b5a1d383b9" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487341 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7-2x}{4} & =3x \\\\ 4(\\frac{7-2x}{4}) & =4\\cdot 3x \\\\ \\cancel{4}(\\frac{7-2x}{\\cancel{4}}) & =12x \\\\ 7-2x & =12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-2x & =12x \\\\ 7-2x+2x & =12x+2x \\\\ 7 & =14x \\\\ 14x & =7 \\\\ \\frac{14x}{14} & =\\frac{7}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{14}x}{\\cancel{14}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487167 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487201 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1069 "q-215513" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1071 "Written in ascending order, the solutions to $4-|2x-5|=3$ are $x=${{field-1}} \nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1072 #uuid "75db5801-eb7a-544e-9657-7c91f7a11bf0" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487342 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-|2x-5| & =3 \\\\ -|2x-5| & =-1 \\\\ |2x-5| & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|2x-5|=2x-5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =1 \\\\ 2x-5 & =1 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x-5|=-(2x-5),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =1 \\\\ -(2x-5) & =1 \\\\ -2x+5 & =1 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487181 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1069 "q-133792" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1071 "![](images/e6/e69bcccc2f30199fd64a9479c623c107610760686c744466736b30995cc4654e.png)\n\nMary is Paul's neighbor. She has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-25$ square yards and whose width is $x+5$ yards, as depicted above. What is the length of Mary's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nMary's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nyards." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1072 #uuid "77006e45-cf97-549c-aab3-71cabbd06eba" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487343 1074 "To find the length of Mary's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-25.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25={x}^{2}-{5}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=(x+5)(x-5)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x-5$ yards.\n\n![](images/1d/1d05c236e65fb4011152735b09dd7668bd6a36b119319a95ec1f3064bd13db4a.png)" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487258 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1069 "q-253831" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1072 #uuid "79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487344 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487240 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1069 "q-147886" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0, & P({A}_{1})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.6, & P({A}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.3, & P({A}_{3})=0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1072 #uuid "855a7cb7-0048-5462-8815-114b332521cc" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487345 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.25+0.25+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)(0.25)}{(0)(0.25)+(0.6)(0.25)+(0.3)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487263 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1069 "q-111426" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1071 "Solve for $w$ where $16{w}^{2}=9.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1072 #uuid "8d4e69dd-adb5-5686-b554-16f7f78bb263" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487346 1074 "First, we isolate the ${w}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{w}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {w}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\ |w| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ |w| & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ w & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487241 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1069 "q-104763" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1071 "Solve for $x$, if $0.6x=-3.6.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1072 #uuid "8d591c49-17a4-5f3d-8812-829122abd153" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487347 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6x & =-3.6 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.6x) & =10\\cdot (-3.6) \\\\ 6x & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & =-36 \\\\ \\frac{6x}{6} & =\\frac{-36}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}x}{\\cancel{6}} & =\\frac{-36}{6} \\\\ x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487166 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1069 "q-44322" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{5y+12}{4}=2y.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1072 #uuid "8fa20dc6-b138-59bb-9538-21dc8c6a5944" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487348 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5y+12}{4} & =2y \\\\ 4(\\frac{5y+12}{4}) & =4\\cdot 2y \\\\ \\cancel{4}(\\frac{5y+12}{\\cancel{4}}) & =8y \\\\ 5y+12 & =8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y+12 & =8y \\\\ 5y+12-5y & =8y-5y \\\\ 12 & =3y \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & =\\frac{3y}{3} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 4 & =y \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487196 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1069 "q-154633" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1071 "If $|7y+3|=1,$ then $y=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1072 #uuid "9491d725-d560-50e8-a347-c4af883e99a8" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487349 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|7y+3|=7y+3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7y+3| & =1 \\\\ 7y+3 & =1 \\\\ 7y & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{7y}{7} & =\\frac{-2}{7} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|7y+3|=-(7y+3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7y+3| & =1 \\\\ -(7y+3) & =1 \\\\ -7y-3 & =1 \\\\ -7y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{-7y}{-7} & =\\frac{4}{-7} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and $y=-\\frac{2}{7}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487266 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1069 "q-253859" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1071 "![](images/37/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1072 #uuid "956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487350 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487185 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1069 "q-130790" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1071 "Given that $2x+6=5x$, what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1072 #uuid "96ac96b0-7a48-5300-b28c-ed2048f4b199" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487351 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $2x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $2x$ from the left-hand side. We do this by subtracting $2x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & =5x \\\\ 2x+6-2x & =5x-2x \\\\ 6 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487190 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1069 "q-350963" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}6\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1072 #uuid "99bfd564-d6cc-54a4-9108-196e20d54d83" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487352 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=6\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 6\\sqrt{x}\\,dx=4{x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}6\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})(4{x}^{3/2}){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{x}^{3/2})(\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[4{e}^{3/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-4(1)^{3/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1)^{2})]-8{\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{e}^{3/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-\\frac{16{x}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =8{e}^{3/2}-(\\frac{16{e}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(24{e}^{3/2}-16{e}^{3/2}+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8{e}^{3/2}+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({e}^{3/2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487205 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1069 "q-75892" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1071 "If $2|5x-3|+1=15,$ then $x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1072 #uuid "99e6cc0a-d678-5e56-8a48-bdcbe16a46ca" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487353 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|5x-3|+1 & =15 \\\\ 2|5x-3| & =14 \\\\ |5x-3| & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|5x-3|=5x-3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x-3| & =7 \\\\ 5x-3 & =7 \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5x-3|=-(5x-3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x-3| & =7 \\\\ -(5x-3) & =7 \\\\ -5x+3 & =7 \\\\ -5x & =4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $x=2.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487140 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1069 "q-148523" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1071 "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1072 #uuid "9b22d29b-922e-508b-b541-5acf3087f769" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487354 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487143 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1069 "q-170685" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1071 "A farm sells its apples to three grocery stores; $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Store $1$ buys $14%$ of the apples.\n\n- Store $2$ buys $26%$ of the apples, and $50%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\n- Store $3$ buys $60%$ of the apples, and $25%$ of those apples are sold in one day.\n\nIf $63%$ of all apples are red, $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, and $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1072 #uuid "9c74ed7c-bf09-54d6-a2a8-5c3ba0194b49" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487355 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that an apple is red, $S$ be the event that the apple is sold in one day, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3}$ be the event that an apple is sold by store $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{1})=0.14 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.26 &  & P(S|{S}_{2})=0.5 \\\\  & P({S}_{3})=0.60 &  & P(S|{S}_{3})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})=0.14+0.26+0.60=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $63%$ of all apples are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.63.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of red apples are sold in one day, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of apples sold in one day are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.45.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple bought by store $1$ is sold in one day, which is represented as $P(S|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(S|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(S|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{(0.63)(0.25)}{P(S|{S}_{1})(0.14)+(0.5)(0.26)+(0.25)(0.60)} \\\\ 0.45 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.45(0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28) & =0.1575 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =\\frac{0.1575}{0.45} \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1})+0.28 & =0.35 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.35-0.28 \\\\ 0.14P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.07 \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.07}{0.14} \\\\ P(S|{S}_{1}) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487152 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1069 "q-180234" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1071 "If $-0.06t=-6.6$, then $t=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1072 #uuid "9d41f481-9aa0-5eac-ac86-1ea37172f582" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487356 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.06t & =-6.6 \\\\ 100\\cdot (-0.06t) & =100\\cdot (-6.6) \\\\ -6t & =-660\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-6$ to isolate $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =-660 \\\\ \\frac{-6t}{-6} & =\\frac{-660}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-6}t}{\\cancel{-6}} & =110 \\\\ t & =110\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487200 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1069 "q-14726" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1071 "$\\int 4{x}^{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1072 #uuid "9e36e24a-9d09-5b52-9d60-57f7f164fe98" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487357 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=4{x}^{7}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 4{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{8}}{2})(\\frac{4}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})}{2}-2\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487193 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1069 "q-516" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1071 "If $\\frac{3x-4}{5}=4$, then $x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1072 #uuid "a03551ce-a9e0-5803-b72e-ac5c9487b91c" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487358 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 5(\\frac{3x-4}{5}) & =5\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{5}(\\frac{3x-4}{\\cancel{5}}) & =20 \\\\ 3x-4 & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4 & =20 \\\\ 3x-4+4 & =20+4 \\\\ 3x & =24 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{24}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & =\\frac{24}{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487220 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1069 "q-252989" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n+2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $A?$\n\n$inf(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1072 #uuid "a2831798-1168-5ea1-b772-79e02ad4074c" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487359 1074 "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(A) & =min\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487273 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1069 "q-181286" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1071 "If $-4-3t=3t-2,$ then $t=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1072 #uuid "a36c0516-4a73-5ecf-b71f-434626a39f6b" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487360 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $-3t$ on the left-hand side and $3t$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $-3t$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by adding $3t$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-3t & =3t-2 \\\\ -4-3t+3t & =3t-2+3t \\\\ -4 & =6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-2$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =6t-2 \\\\ -4+2 & =6t-2+2 \\\\ -2 & =6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =6t \\\\ \\frac{(-2)}{6} & =\\frac{6t}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\cancel{6}t}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =t \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487262 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1069 "q-132418" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1072 #uuid "a3c08bbb-13dd-5937-9a36-d4dc386d9f8f" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487361 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[4y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487212 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1069 "q-132427" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1071 "![](images/01/019f0f9f83ddf323d543d109677335cb3ae72a200baf834453a0eca16a9353ba.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}6y\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1072 #uuid "a42208da-aa4c-5f1b-858e-ec1e99355b8d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487362 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d222e86f6af7c3ed2b35711114c9062d6f0c9b0f2551aa44cac33318c6e5813f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,x+2\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\le y\\le x+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}6y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}6y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}6y\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}6y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}/2+2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}/2+2}^{x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3[{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2)}^{2}-{(x+2)}^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3[{(x+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2)}^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{x}^{2}-4x)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-{x}^{2}+4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{20}+{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}){|}_{-2}^{0}+(-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{20}-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{3(-2)^{5}}{20}+(-2)^{3}-6(-2)^{2})-\\frac{3(2)^{5}}{20}-(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}+8+24-\\frac{24}{5}-8+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487272 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1069 "q-253525" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1072 #uuid "a4eaf9cf-b8c1-5f93-bf45-1dc2cc8b5b67" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487363 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487178 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1069 "q-148079" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1071 "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1072 #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487364 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487245 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1069 "q-215504" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1071 "Consider the absolute value equation \n\n$$\n-8|2x+1|+20=4.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|2x+1|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1072 #uuid "aacab06e-bd32-550c-b949-9f30eb343d4e" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487365 1074 "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we subtract $20$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8|2x+1|+20 & =4 \\\\ -8|2x+1|+20-20 & =4-20 \\\\ -8|2x+1| & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8|2x+1| & =-16 \\\\ \\frac{-8|2x+1|}{-8} & =\\frac{-16}{-8} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-8}|2x+1|}{\\cancel{-8}} & =2 \\\\ |2x+1| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487257 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1069 "q-253910" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1072 #uuid "aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487366 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487283 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1069 "q-148273" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers that play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play the piano, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $15%$ of teenagers play the violin.\n\n- $65%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $35%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $12%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, and $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that plays the violin is good at math? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1072 #uuid "ad4190f4-bdf3-5375-9c0a-f6bde72a3f1c" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487367 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.65 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.2+0.15+0.65=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $12%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.12.\n$$\n\n- Since $27%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.27.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of students who are good at math started to play an instrument before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|M)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who plays the violin is good at math, which is represented as $P(M|{T}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\ P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{(0.27)(0.12)}{(0.2)(0.6)+P(M|{T}_{2})(0.15)+(0.35)(0.65)} \\\\ 0.08 & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(M|{T}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.08(0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2})) & =0.0324 \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0324}{0.08} \\\\ 0.3475+0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200} \\\\ 0.15P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475 \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.15}(\\frac{81}{200}-0.3475) \\\\ P(M|{T}_{2}) & ≈0.383\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487217 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1069 "q-215502" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1071 "Consider the absolute value equation \n\n$$\n8-|4x+5|=1.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|4x+5|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1072 #uuid "ad83e0ea-4391-59a0-adc0-7a2eea885dc7" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487368 1074 "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we subtract $8$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-|4x+5|=1 \\\\ 8-|4x+5|-8=1-8 \\\\ -|4x+5|=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|4x+5| & =-7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-|4x+5|) & =(-1)\\cdot (-7) \\\\ (\\cancel{-}1)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}|4x+5|) & =7 \\\\ |4x+5| & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487146 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1069 "q-154692" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1071 "If $|\\frac{7}{5}z+3|=1,$ then $z=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1072 #uuid "aebd4e07-ea29-59de-8b1b-1c21cbfc61d2" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487369 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|\\frac{7}{5}z+3|=\\frac{7}{5}z+3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{5}z+3| & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}z+3 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}z & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{5}z & =-2 \\\\ (\\frac{5}{7})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{5}z) & =(\\frac{5}{7})\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{5}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{7}{5}}z) & =-\\frac{10}{7} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|\\frac{7}{5}z+3|=-(\\frac{7}{5}z+3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{7}{5}z+3| & =1 \\\\ -(\\frac{7}{5}z+3) & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}z-3 & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{5}z & =4 \\\\ (-\\frac{5}{7})\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}z) & =(-\\frac{5}{7})\\cdot (4) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{5}{7}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{7}{5}}z) & =-\\frac{20}{7} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{20}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{20}{7}$ and $z=-\\frac{10}{7}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487246 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1069 "q-156084" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1071 "If $|3x|=9,$ then $x=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1072 #uuid "af6d2532-61ef-5940-9d1b-a188a8fed3c0" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487370 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|3x|=3x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x| & =9 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|3x|=-3x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x| & =9 \\\\ -3x & =9 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & =\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\pm 3.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487162 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1069 "q-181274" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1071 "If $10x-3=5+6x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1072 #uuid "b03a4399-665a-560d-811a-3bccaf2d3f58" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487371 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $10x$ on the left-hand side and $6x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $6x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $6x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-3 & =5+6x \\\\ 10x-3-6x & =5+6x-6x \\\\ 4x-3 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3 & =5 \\\\ 4x-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ 4x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487197 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1069 "q-51617" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1071 "If $0.35q-0.3=-1$, then $q=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1072 #uuid "b69ac07e-a3e5-5cb5-8868-26a8f07d7004" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487372 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.35q-0.3 & =-1 \\\\ 100(0.35q-0.3) & =100(-1) \\\\ 35q-30 & =-100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation using the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35q-30 & =-100 \\\\ 35q-30+30 & =-100+30 \\\\ 35q & =-70 \\\\ \\frac{35q}{35} & =\\frac{-70}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{35}q}{\\cancel{35}} & =-\\frac{70}{35} \\\\ q & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487215 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1069 "q-101642" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1071 "Solve for $t$ where $3(2t-1)=\\frac{4t-3}{2}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1072 #uuid "ba37a28b-262f-5934-93da-824b60873e74" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487373 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2t-1) & =\\frac{4t-3}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (3(2t-1)) & =2(\\frac{4t-3}{2}) \\\\ 6(2t-1) & =\\cancel{2}(\\frac{4t-3}{\\cancel{2}}) \\\\ 12t-6 & =4t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-6 & =4t-3 \\\\ 12t-6-4t & =-3+4t-4t \\\\ 8t-6 & =-3 \\\\ 8t-6+6 & =-3+6 \\\\ 8t & =3 \\\\ \\frac{8t}{8} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487148 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1069 "q-133797" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1071 "![](images/60/60e3ad251f3b7eed158489e287b072ee133955841c812da26f37538a539f9cd7.png)\n\nWhen $x=20\\,\\text{yd},$ the dimensions of Karol's plot of land are $24\\,\\text{yd}$ and $18\\text{yd}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nyards." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1072 #uuid "bb8e2d93-f5db-5ed4-9595-24f5393bca59" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487374 1074 "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=24\\,\\text{yd}$  and $b=18\\,\\text{yd}.$\n\n![](images/35/35dff3715d9e7e06f7c15a3b80c0171820f71e1998695550a7672377d981ee76.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{24}^{2}+{18}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{576+324} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{900} \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487172 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487236 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487252 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487254 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1069 "q-253556" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1071 "Consider the set $C=(-2,-1].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(C).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $C$ is the {{field-1}} of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1072 #uuid "be9aa3b1-68a0-5981-aa70-f10a99f6fbac" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487375 1074 "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $C$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $C.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $C.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $C.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $C$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $C,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(C)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the maximum of $C.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $C$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(C)=-1\\in C.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487224 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1069 "q-128811" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1071 "If $-3(1-p)=4(p+1),$ then $p=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1072 #uuid "c3b531c0-4761-5340-bef2-0f6d109a9d03" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487376 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(1-p) & =4(p+1) \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot p & =4\\cdot p+4\\cdot 1 \\\\ -3+3p & =4p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $3p$ on the left-hand side and $4p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $3p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $3p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+3p & =4p+4 \\\\ -3+3p-3p & =4p+4-3p \\\\ -3 & =p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we remove the $4$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p+4 \\\\ -3-4 & =p+4-4 \\\\ -7 & =p \\\\ p & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487187 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487223 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1069 "q-323212" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-4=3{t}^{2},\\,y+2=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1072 #uuid "c4ed1fe6-d320-5a32-bfb8-73846e898159" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487377 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x-4}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y+2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+2}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x-4}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x-4}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =36\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =12(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487211 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1069 "q-84852" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1072 #uuid "c50b5890-601e-5b61-a526-2b2221f83863" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487378 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3},\\,t=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2(y+2)}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =3(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487239 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1069 "q-133791" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1071 "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Paul's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nyards." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1072 #uuid "c5c07663-c2ba-5968-924a-8ce6b0b341c8" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487379 1074 "The width of the plot is $x+5$ yards, and its area is $3{x}^{2}+15x$ square yards. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(3{x}^{2},15x)=3x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+15x & = \\\\ (3x\\cdot x)+(3x\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x\\cdot x)+(3x\\cdot 5) & = \\\\ 3x(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $3x$ yards.\n\n![](images/13/13d17ee1a2e1157f7cf0afa191f2c4096c7c6dea6086f0b3aafd48af159aeb1a.png)" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487277 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1069 "q-147887" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=\\frac{2}{5}, & P({A}_{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1072 #uuid "c768c6df-9213-55c1-bcb4-06af864aa6e9" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487380 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})+(0)(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{40})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487281 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1069 "q-75890" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1071 "If $|6-2b|+2=18,$ then $b=$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1072 #uuid "c8460d4b-81b8-5707-a316-5df813e7f65d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487381 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6-2b|+2 & =18 \\\\ |6-2b| & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|6-2b|=6-2b,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6-2b| & =16 \\\\ 6-2b & =16 \\\\ -2b & =10 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|6-2b|=-(6-2b),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6-2b| & =16 \\\\ -(6-2b) & =16 \\\\ 2b-6 & =16 \\\\ 2b & =22 \\\\ b & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $b=-5$ and $b=11.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487284 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1069 "q-132514" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1072 #uuid "cbaf5fee-0009-5eb1-a042-591a8b53019b" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487382 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/8c/8c55ebba1610708143086512788e64464e8b50a517257bd63a1c28b89ee7327d.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=6-x & \\,⟹\\,x=6-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =6-y \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & =(6-y)^{2} \\\\ y & =36-12y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-13y+36 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $y=9$ since it does not satisfy the first equality. Therefore, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/c9/c9c3e3bdec1a007c47d8d1251b790b970a0d367304a5b1e5244bec014e69580d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 6-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487159 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1069 "q-133794" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1071 "![](images/7a/7ad6be914ed14fb22276982edeab75c9726c56e57e32760371db7936b8091468.png)\n\nPaul's neighbor to the south, Karol, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+2x-8$ square yards, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Karol's plot of land is $x+4$ yards, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nKarol's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nyards." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1072 #uuid "cbff1985-d682-5038-ad53-7d9761aafbae" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487383 1074 "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+2x-8.$\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-8).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 8 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-4) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-8) & =-7 & \\times  \\\\ -1+8 & =7 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-4) & =-2 & \\times  \\\\ -2+4 & =2 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $2x=4x-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x-2x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x-2x-8 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(-2x-8) & = \\\\ x(x+4)-2(x+4) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ yards. \n\n![](images/b7/b7bff7b60f02cab37aef8f40d7b303627aedcc37995afc4ffbc2fe756110673a.png)" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487157 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1069 "q-133793" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1071 "![](images/c5/c57074e325bd05bdfd076a8f07b50b15c18099fc37bcace725201237d8034a1c.png)\n\nA utility pole is located on the edge of Paul's land, as shown above. The distance from the right edge of Paul's plot to the pole is $2x-4$ yards. Find the distance to the pole from the left edge as a function of $x.$\n\nThe required distance is \n{{field-1}}\nyards." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1072 #uuid "cfee4982-2b49-58b3-8a10-a19a8ff05ec4" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487384 1074 "In part 1, we found that the length of Paul's plot is $3x$ yards.\n\nTo find the required distance, we need to subtract polynomials $3x$ and $2x-4:$\n\nWe put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3-2)x+4 & = \\\\ x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487210 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487228 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1069 "q-215509" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1071 "Written in ascending order, the solutions to $|5-x|-8=7$ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1072 #uuid "d1182683-804f-5b60-aecd-25a7c3c4b698" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487385 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-x|-8 & =7 \\\\ |5-x| & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|5-x|=5-x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-x| & =15 \\\\ 5-x & =15 \\\\ -x & =10 \\\\ x & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5-x|=-(5-x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-x| & =15 \\\\ -(5-x) & =15 \\\\ -5+x & =15 \\\\ x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-10$ and $x=20.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487259 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1069 "q-84846" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+5)?$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1072 #uuid "da2c9d27-2db1-52e6-a2e3-b28e1798b755" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487386 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-5,-2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p(x+5) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y+2=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487165 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487221 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1069 "q-215130" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1071 "Written in **ascending order**, the solutions to $|6-k|=2$ are $k=${{field-1}} and $k=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1072 #uuid "db6832f5-3aa6-5721-9509-53750f84af47" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487387 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|6-k|=6-k,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6-k| & =2 \\\\ 6-k & =2 \\\\ -k & =-4 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|6-k|=-(6-k),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6-k| & =2 \\\\ -(6-k) & =2 \\\\ -6+k & =2 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $k=4$ and $k=8.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487195 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1069 "q-107857" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1072 #uuid "dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487388 1074 "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487274 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1069 "q-351045" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1072 #uuid "dec6c4d2-4bd8-5abe-a87c-7b60b0c36fa7" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487389 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x(\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{log}_{5}⁡(({e}^{3})^{3})\\,({e}^{3})-{log}_{5}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡({e}^{9})-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3}+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487161 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1069 "q-253543" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1071 "What is the supremum of the set $B=\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}?$\n\n$sup(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1072 #uuid "df0bc2ee-bd6a-5b2e-965b-ef9ca16078ea" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487390 1074 "The supremum of a set $B$ is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the supremum of $B$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(B) & =max\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487179 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1069 "q-107859" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1072 #uuid "df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487391 1074 "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487170 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1069 "q-154670" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1071 "Which of the following equations has *no* solution?\n\nI. $|5-x|=7$\n\nII. $1-|5-x|=5$\n\nIII. $|5-x|=-1$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1072 #uuid "e251dfd2-42a3-5421-a165-7d060c5b7a7c" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487392 1074 "An absolute value equation has no solution if, when the absolute value is isolated on the left side, the right-hand side is negative.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each equation in turn:\n\n- Equation I has a solution. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is non-negative.\n\n- Equation II *has no solution*. To see this, we first isolate the absolute value on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-|5-x| & =5 \\\\ -|5-x| & =4 \\\\ |5-x| & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side is *negative*, this equation has *no* solution.\n\n- Equation III *has no solution*. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487214 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1069 "q-12571" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1072 #uuid "e2daa4c1-fd00-5b69-bf1e-328646061d80" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487393 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(5x))(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487199 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1069 "q-103278" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1071 "![](images/62/62412006e358dd798b602547e22eb63c9c274b0d740df885e9c69a1af06bcb97.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the angles shown above?\n\nI. $m∠AOB=m∠COD$\n\nII. $m∠BOC=m∠EOF$\n\nIII. $m∠COD=m∠DOE$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1072 #uuid "e51f05e0-1186-559b-935a-5d0ce775e04e" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487394 1074 "From the diagram, we see that \n\n- $∠AOB$ and $∠EOF$ are marked with $|$,\n\n- $∠BOC$ and $∠DOE$ are marked with $||$, and\n\n- $∠COD$ is marked with $|||.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOB & ≅∠EOF \\\\ ∠BOC & ≅∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠EOF \\\\ m∠BOC & =m∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the statements listed is true." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487271 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1069 "q-89549" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1072 #uuid "e726e2b9-ad90-5a69-bca6-00fc64c93333" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487395 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487176 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487188 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487206 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487209 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487231 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487238 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1069 "q-253041" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1071 "Consider the set $A=[0,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1072 #uuid "e871b676-2c06-50d7-81b0-8662a3a9169b" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487396 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le 0$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,0]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded above, $A$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=0.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487219 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1069 "q-181250" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1071 "If $17y=9y-16$, then $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1072 #uuid "ee568a57-5aea-5924-963c-e0b1b71db69f" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487397 1074 "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $17y$ on the left-hand side and $9y$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $9y$ from the right-hand side. We do this by subtracting $9y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17y & =9y-16 \\\\ 17y-9y & =9y-16-9y \\\\ 8y & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y & =-16 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & =\\frac{(-16)}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}y}{\\cancel{8}} & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487154 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1069 "q-342572" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1071 "![](images/b1/b1735679cbf165e26ac22f1fd3076d450231ea8c20c1905ec74f008bc89ec33a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1072 #uuid "f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487398 1074 "Since $(2,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487139 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487189 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487204 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487222 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487232 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487276 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1069 "q-253561" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1071 "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\pi \\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1072 #uuid "f25266f1-2830-5a2e-9d12-da4f0e084a1d" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487399 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\pi $ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\pi $ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\pi ,\\infty )=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\pi .$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487244 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1069 "q-44121" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1071 "Solve  $\\frac{{q}^{2}}{2}-18=0.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1072 #uuid "f380333b-76ae-58b4-8fc8-adf9bbb2d3ab" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487400 1074 "First, we isolate the ${q}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{q}^{2}}{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{q}^{2}}{2} & =18 \\\\ {q}^{2} & =2\\cdot 18 \\\\ {q}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{q}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |q| & =6 \\\\ q & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487253 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1069 "q-171587" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1071 "If $7(x-2)=3(x+2)+2x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1072 #uuid "f616f4fb-ccb3-5ccf-8dc2-aabb1a45bb95" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487401 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x-2) & =3(x+2)+2x \\\\ 7\\cdot x-7\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x+3\\cdot 2+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =3x+6+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =6+3x+2x \\\\ 7x-14 & =6+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $7x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $5x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $5x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-14 & =6+5x \\\\ 7x-14-5x & =6+5x-5x \\\\ 2x-14 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-14$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding $14$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-14 & =6 \\\\ 2x-14+14 & =6+14 \\\\ 2x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =20 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{20}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487249 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1069 "q-148085" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1071 "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1072 #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487402 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487194 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1069 "q-159358" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1071 "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1,$ itinerary $2,$ and itinerary $3.$ For each itinerary, customers can decide to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $60%$ of the customers, and $10%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers, and $25%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $20%$ of the customers.\n\nIf $30%$ of all customers travel with children, $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, and $83%$ of customers that choose the bus travel with children, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ takes the bus? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1072 #uuid "fa54d1c3-840f-55dc-9c9a-7cb1c9435baf" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487403 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a customer has children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and ${I}_{1},{I}_{2},{I}_{3}$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({I}_{1})=0.6 &  & P(B|{I}_{1})=0.1 \\\\  & P({I}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(B|{I}_{2})=0.25 \\\\  & P({I}_{3})=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${I}_{1}⊔{I}_{2}⊔{I}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({I}_{1})+P({I}_{2})+P({I}_{3})=0.6+0.2+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of customers have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $50%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|C)=0.5.\n$$\n\n- Since $83%$ of customers that choose the bus have children, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|B)=0.83.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses itinerary $3$ travels by bus, which is represented as $P(B|{I}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(C|B) & =\\frac{P(B|C)P(C)}{P(B|{I}_{1})P({I}_{1})+P(B|{I}_{2})P({I}_{2})+P(B|{I}_{3})P({I}_{3})} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{(0.1)(0.6)+(0.25)(0.2)+P(B|{I}_{3})(0.2)} \\\\ 0.83 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{I}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.83(0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.83} \\\\ 0.11+0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83} \\\\ 0.2P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{15}{83}-0.11 \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.2}(\\frac{15}{83}-0.11) \\\\ P(B|{I}_{3}) & ≈0.354\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487171 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1069 "q-103249" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1071 "![](images/cb/cb2cda27c9ca2744c059279e853969858a7b3d1882cde343177badc7aa33145d.png)\n\nWhich of the angles shown above are congruent?" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1072 #uuid "fbfe98b2-d913-5b5f-9244-1e7d8b4485eb" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487404 1074 "From the diagram, we see that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & ={25}^{∘} \\\\ m∠2 & ={75}^{∘} \\\\ m∠3 & ={75}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠2 & ≅∠3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487203 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1069 "q-253912" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1072 #uuid "fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487405 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/8b/8be6d8824c8cbf1fb66d64d3ccc027f2949149adc9f95cf4d1f3a814baec80b3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $-1.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-1\\notin A.\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487285 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1069 "q-12797" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1071 "Find $b$ if ${b}^{2}-15=4.$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1072 #uuid "fe6cba18-c737-5fde-a708-668f780bcd21" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487406 1074 "First, we isolate the ${b}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-15 & =4 \\\\ {b}^{2} & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{b}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{19} \\\\ |b| & =\\sqrt{19} \\\\ b & =\\pm \\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487407 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487420 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487407 1062 #uuid "02dcebbb-e0ab-58c9-9244-58670d1f8782" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487407 1063 #edb/ref 17592186487361 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487408 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487427 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487408 1062 #uuid "05319ee0-3f80-50c8-bc7c-953e1146c0d0" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487408 1063 #edb/ref 17592186487324 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487409 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487411 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487409 1062 #uuid "091d4923-4cda-500f-b9f4-8ac1e9d91cd5" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487409 1063 #edb/ref 17592186351405 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487410 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487408 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487410 1062 #uuid "117ee75c-7c36-545e-9458-12a962def7e5" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487410 1063 #edb/ref 17592186487312 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487411 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487442 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487411 1062 #uuid "11f534ed-c297-588d-85ea-334d9dacbc9e" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487411 1063 #edb/ref 17592186487337 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487412 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487440 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487412 1062 #uuid "2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487412 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172230 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487413 1058 #edb/ref 17592186487425 13194139535256 17592186045417 true] [17592186487413 1062 #uuid "29ee00b7-02cb-5105-a7be-7bc30258a733" 13194139535256 17592186045417 